### a) Algorithm to Add an Item After a Given Node `LOC` (5 Marks)

```ascii
  Before:  HEAD --> ... --> [ LOC | * ] ----------> [ NEXT | * ] --> ...
  Step 4:                   [ LOC | * ]   [ NEW | * ] --^
  Step 5:                   [ LOC | * ] --> [ NEW | * ] --> [ NEXT | * ]
```

```text
Algorithm SLL_INSERT_AFTER(HEAD, KEY, ITEM)
[Inserts ITEM immediately after the first node storing KEY]

1. [Locate Target Node LOC]
   Set LOC = HEAD
   While LOC != NULL and LOC->data != KEY do
       LOC = LOC->next
2. If LOC == NULL then
       Write "GIVEN NODE NOT FOUND" and Return HEAD
3. [Allocate New Node]
   Allocate NEW from heap memory
   If NEW == NULL then
       Write "MEMORY OVERFLOW" and Return HEAD
   Set NEW->data = ITEM
4. [Splice NEW After LOC — Order Is Critical]
   Set NEW->next = LOC->next
   Set LOC->next = NEW
5. Return HEAD
```

- **Critical Pointer Order:** `NEW->next = LOC->next` **must** execute *before* `LOC->next = NEW`; reversing them overwrites `LOC->next` first and orphans the entire remainder of the list. Runs in **`O(1)` time** if pointer `LOC` is already given (`O(n)` if searched by `KEY`).

### b) Algorithm & C Function to Count Number of Nodes (5 Marks)

```text
Algorithm SLL_COUNT_NODES(HEAD)
1. Set COUNT = 0
2. Set PTR = HEAD
3. While PTR != NULL do
       COUNT = COUNT + 1
       PTR   = PTR->next
4. Return COUNT
```

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
};

int countNodes(const struct Node *head) {
    int count = 0;
    for (const struct Node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next) {
        count++;
    }
    return count;
}

int main(void) {
    struct Node n3 = {30, NULL}, n2 = {20, &n3}, n1 = {10, &n2};
    printf("Count = %d\n", countNodes(&n1));
    return 0;
}
```

- **Edge Case & Complexity:** Correctly returns `0` when `HEAD == NULL`; visits each of the `n` nodes once in **`O(n)` time** and **`O(1)` auxiliary space**.

